# Line endings.
#
# core.autocrlf=true already stores LF in the repository; what it also does is
# add a CR to every line on checkout. That makes the working tree bigger than
# the thing that actually ships, and one test measures the working tree:
# tests/artifact-budget.test.js caps the HTML shell at 175 KB via fs.statSync.
#
# index.html is 178,636 bytes in git and in production, comfortably under the
# 179,200 cap, and 180,570 bytes on a Windows checkout. The 1,934 byte
# difference is exactly its 1,934 CRs. So the budget test passed on macOS and
# Linux and failed on Windows for the same commit, and the file it was
# complaining about was never over budget.
#
# Pinning LF makes every checkout byte-for-byte identical to what is deployed.
# Nothing in the repository changes: the stored blobs were already LF.
*.html  text eol=lf
*.js    text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.csv   text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf

# PowerShell on Windows is happier with CRLF, and no budget measures it.
*.ps1   text eol=crlf

# Never touched.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.xlsx  binary
